---
import Layout from '~/layouts/OpenSourceLayout.astro';
import Impact from '~/components/forma/Impact.astro';
import Rankings from '~/components/open-source/Rankings.astro';
import impact from '~/data/open-source-impact.json';
import ranks from '~/data/gitranks.json';
import { dateLabel, gitRanks } from '~/data/open-source';
import { getCanonical } from '~/utils/permalinks';
const meta = {
	title: 'Open-source impact & rankings — Liran Tal',
	description:
		'Dated npm download and GitHub contribution data, repository stars, and GitRanks context for Liran Tal’s open-source work, with source links and scope.',
	canonical: getCanonical('/open-source/impact'),
};
---

<Layout {meta}>
	<section class="oss-intro" aria-labelledby="impact-title">
		<h1 id="impact-title">Impact, with context.</h1>
		<p>
			Downloads show how packages get installed. Stars show interest. Merged pull requests show accepted changes.
			Together they tell more of the story than any single counter.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section class="oss-dashboard" aria-label="Package and GitHub impact"><Impact /></section>
	<section class="oss-section" aria-labelledby="breadth-title">
		<div class="oss-section-heading">
			<h2 id="breadth-title">The breadth of upstream work</h2><a href="/open-source/contributions"
				>Read selected contribution stories</a
			>
		</div>
		<dl class="oss-stats">
			<div>
				<dt>Merged PRs outside my namespace</dt><dd>{impact.mergedPullRequests.total.toLocaleString('en-US')}</dd>
			</div>
			<div><dt>Repositories those PRs reached</dt><dd>{impact.mergedPullRequests.repositories}</dd></div>
			<div><dt>On GitHub since</dt><dd>{impact.profile.joined.slice(0, 4)}</dd></div>
		</dl>
		<p class="oss-caption">
			As of {dateLabel(impact.asOf)}. Includes organization and work-related repositories; not a count of exclusively
			volunteer work.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section class="oss-section" id="gitranks" aria-labelledby="gitranks-title">
		<div class="oss-section-heading">
			<h2 id="gitranks-title">Top 0.1% worldwide on GitRanks</h2><a href={gitRanks.profile}
				>Explore my GitRanks profile</a
			>
		</div>
		<p>
			In the captured GitRanks snapshot, my profile is in the top 0.1% worldwide in all three rankings. The contributor
			score reflects the reach of projects I contributed to: over a million stars across 89 repositories, rather than
			stars earned by my own code.
		</p>
		<Rankings />
		<p class="oss-caption">
			Contributor rank counts each repository owned by others once, measuring reach rather than PR volume. GitRanks’ 89
			contributed-to repositories have different coverage from the 193 in the direct GitHub search above. Its
			owned-repository star total also covers more than the six featured repositories in the dashboard. See <a
				href={gitRanks.methodology}>GitRanks’ methodology</a
			> for inclusion rules and refresh frequency; the independent service does not count private contributions.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section class="oss-section" id="languages" aria-labelledby="languages-title">
		<div class="oss-section-heading">
			<h2 id="languages-title">Language rankings</h2><a href="https://gitranks.com/profile/lirantal/languages"
				>All language ranks on GitRanks</a
			>
		</div>
		<p>
			The same snapshot places me #1 in Israel for both JavaScript and PHP. Here are three languages spanning my
			open-source work, from longstanding network tools to Node.js and AI developer tooling.
		</p>
		<table class="oss-language-table">
			<caption class="sr-only">Selected GitRanks language positions, captured {dateLabel(ranks.capturedAt)}</caption>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">Language</th><th scope="col">Worldwide</th><th scope="col">Israel</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody
				>{
					ranks.languages.map((language) => (
						<tr>
							<>
								<th scope="row">{language.name}</th>
								<td>
									<>
										<a href={`https://gitranks.com/language/${language.name}/global/1`}>
											#{language.global.position.toLocaleString('en-US')}
										</a>
										<span>{language.global.percentileLabel}</span>
									</>
								</td>
								<td>
									<>
										<a href={`https://gitranks.com/language/${language.name}/Israel/1`}>#{language.israel.position}</a>
										<span>{language.israel.percentileLabel}</span>
									</>
								</td>
							</>
						</tr>
					))
				}</tbody
			>
		</table>
		<p class="oss-caption">
			Language rankings distribute stars on owned repositories by their language breakdown; they are not based on lines
			I personally wrote. Country ranks use GitHub profile locations and apply only to the profiles GitRanks includes.
			Source: <a href="https://gitranks.com/profile/lirantal/languages/country">GitRanks language rankings for Israel</a
			> and <a href={gitRanks.methodology}>methodology</a>.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section class="oss-section" id="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title">
		<div class="oss-section-heading"><h2 id="sources-title">What the numbers cover</h2></div>
		<div class="oss-measures">
			<article>
				<h3>Package downloads</h3><p>
					A common dated window for five selected maintained npm packages, including their dependency installs. These
					are downloads, not unique developers, and not a lifetime total. Follow each package’s linked API record in the
					dashboard for the exact window.
				</p>
			</article>
			<article>
				<h3>Repository stars & forks</h3><p>
					A dated snapshot of six featured repositories. Stars and forks are separate measures of interest and reuse.
					They do not represent all of my projects, unique people, or production deployments.
				</p>
			</article>
			<article>
				<h3>Yearly GitHub contributions</h3><p>
					The total displayed on my public GitHub contribution calendar for the stated year and snapshot date. GitHub
					combines qualifying commits, issues, pull requests, and reviews; this is not a commit count or a count of
					changes merged upstream.
				</p>
			</article>
			<article>
				<h3>Merged upstream PRs</h3><p>
					The public GitHub search <code>{impact.mergedPullRequests.query}</code> returned {
						impact.mergedPullRequests.total
					} unique PRs over {impact.mergedPullRequests.pagesFetched} pages, with complete results. The repository total counts
					distinct repositories in those results. The namespace filter includes organizations I work with, including Snyk.
				</p><p>
					<a href={impact.mergedPullRequests.source}>Repeat the GitHub search</a> · <a
						href={impact.mergedPullRequests.apiSource}>GitHub API source</a
					>
				</p>
			</article>
		</div>
		<p class="oss-caption">
			Snapshots are collected at different times and measure different things, so their totals should not be added
			together. None of these numbers is fetched when you visit the site. <a href="/recognitions#milestones-heading"
				>Archived GitHub Trending appearances</a
			> have their own dates, language filters, and evidence.
		</p>
	</section>
</Layout>
